in this video we will discuss the Coombs  test and before discussing the Coombs  test it is very important to understand  the concept of the autoantibody so this  is an RPC and on the surface of the RPC  there are lots of antigens for example  the [ __ ] blood grouping antigens the RH  antigens and many others and some  conditions of the body for example the  immuno hemolytic anemias the body  produces certain types of antibodies  which are directed against these  antigens and these antibodies attach to  these antigens and cause immune mediated  destruction of the RBC's and lead to  anemia and this antibody is known as the  or xx body and the Coombs test is a test  which is done to detect these types of  antibodies so there are two types of  Coombs test the direct type and the  indirect type and the common thing in  both of them is the Coombs reagent and  it is very important to understand the  Coombs reagent how it is made what is it  to understand the basics of the Coombs  test so let's first understand how is  the Coombs reagent made please excuse me  for my drawings but let's suppose this  is a human and the body of this human  contains lots of these Auto antibodies  so what we do is we take the blood from  this human and we separate the serum  from this blood which contains all of  these or 20 bodies and what we do next  is that we inject this serum into an  animal please don't ask me whatever  animal is this it just is now the immune  system of this animal recognizes these  antibodies as foreign and produces its  own antibodies against these and what we  do next is we take the blood of that  animal and separate those antibodies  which the animal has just produced  against these or 20 bodies and we  harvest those antibodies and this is the  Coombs reagent so the Coombs reagent is  nothing but the antibodies which the  body of the animal has produced against  the  Auto antibodies let's first talk about  the direct Coombs test and out of the  two types of the kun test this is the  one which is used to detect the immuno  hemolytic anemias so let's suppose this  is a human which has immuno hemolytic  anemia and his blood contains Auto  antibodies which are directed against  his RBC's so what we do is we take the  blood sample from this patient and we  put that blood into a test tube or a  beaker so here are the RBC's which are  present in the blood of this patient and  here are those antibodies which are  attached to the RBC surface and to keep  things simple for the rest of the video  I will refer to these Auto antibodies as  blue antibodies and the antibodies which  are present in the Coombs reagent as  green an tea parties what we do next is  we add the Coombs reagent which has  these anti Auto antibodies or the green  antibodies into this blood sample now  since these green antibodies are  directed against these blue antibodies  when they react they call the attachment  of these blue antibodies and since in  turn these blue antibodies are bound to  the RBC's it forms a gluten ated mass of  the RBC's at the bottom of the test tube  or the beaker so here is the  agglutinated mass of the RBC's and this  is a visible reaction which confirms the  presence of Auto antibodies in the blood  sample and one of the very important  condition in which the direct Coombs  test is done is the hemolytic disease of  the newborn which is also a type of  immune or humidity anemia now let's talk  about the indirect type of the Coombs  test and this test is mainly done for  the prenatal screening of females for  detection of RH antibodies sorry this is  a writing mistake this is antibody not  antigen let's suppose this is a pregnant  female and again it excuse me for my  fantastic drawings and let's suppose  this is a fetus inside her womb the  female is Rh negative and the fetus is  Rh positive so what happens is sometimes  at the time of the pasteurization the  fetal blood comes in contact with a  maternal blood and  maternal blood develops 